detail.abstract:
Classical general equilibrium models (Walras, Arrow-Debreu) focus on the static existence of equilibrium, placing the coordination process outside the scope of analysis. Dynamic approaches (complexity theory, recursive macro-models) either rely on strong optimization assumptions or are limited to computational simulations without explicit analytical stability conditions. In this article, we overcome these limitations by conducting an in-depth dynamic analysis of the Symmetric Model of General Economic Equilibrium previously proposed by us [12, 13, 15].&nbsp;
<div>
 Unlike its initial conceptual-architectonic presentation, we for the first time perform a complete mathematical formalization of the model as a recursive dynamic system. Based on this, the following key results, constituting the scientific novelty of the work, are obtained:&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 1. A theorem on the dynamic unattainability of equilibrium is proven. It is shown that when accounting for time lags in the transmission of price signals between interconnected product and resource markets, the adaptive process leads not to convergence but to sustained oscillations.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 2. Explicit analytical conditions for the system's transition into an oscillatory regime (endogenous cycle) are derived, linked to behavioral parameters (price adjustment speed, demand inertia). Thereby, cyclicality is elevated from the realm of empirical observations to that of logical consequences of the reproduction structure.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 3. The robustness of the mechanism is demonstrated through a series of model extensions (money, foreign trade, multi-level production), proving that dynamic instability is a systemic property and not an artifact of the base specification. Thus, the contribution of the article lies not in proposing a new model, but in its profound analytical development, revealing fundamentally new content: the model serves not to find equilibrium, but to explain its fundamental unattainability and to uncover the endogenous causes of macroeconomic cyclicality.
</div>
